    "q-221722": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221722",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $11\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-6?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[-17]$\n\nIII. $[-8]$",
      "worked_solution": "We have $-6\\in [x]$ if $-6∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\,|\\,(-6-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-6\\in [5]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\in [-17]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6+17)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\notin [-8]$ because $11∤(-6+8)\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
