    "q-221800": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221800",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={0}^{4}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={1}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={2}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=16} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={3}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=81} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={4}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=256} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
