    "q-221739": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221739",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,20},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $5∣(b-a).$   How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣a} \\\\  & ={0,5,10,15,20} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-1)} \\\\  & ={1,6,11,16} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-2)} \\\\  & ={2,7,12,17} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-3)} \\\\  & ={3,8,13,18} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼4} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-4)} \\\\  & ={4,9,14,19} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,5,10,15,20},\\,[1]={1,6,11,16},\\,[2]={2,7,12,17},\\,[3]={3,8,13,18},\\,[4]={4,9,14,19}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected.",
