    "q-221801": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221801",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2},$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=0} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $3$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected.",
